<!--
    kuna issue `kuna-foldcallretphi` (P6, sub-stage explicit-marking).

    `foldcallret` (default-on) lets an order-safe single-use call output fall
    through ActionMarkExplicit::baseExplicit's forced-explicit call arm, but
    ActionMarkImplied re-tests the candidate in checkImpliedCover.  Its
    Merge::inflateTest arm forces a value explicit when one of its operands has
    another live SSA version of the same HighVariable over the candidate's
    cover.  Upstream never reaches that arm with a call output (Ghidra marks
    every call output explicit one pass earlier), and the version it collides
    with here is the CPUI_INDIRECT the call ITSELF attaches to the global it
    reads as an argument, a call may write any global.  So passing a global to
    a call is enough to block the fold, even though the fold moves the operand
    read and that write together.

    `foldcallretphi` (default-on) discounts exactly that self-inflicted
    collision.  foldcallret's own order-safety predicate still applies: no
    call, load, store or callother between the call and its single use, and
    no write to persistent or address-tied storage anywhere from the call to
    the statement the folded expression is printed in (GH-657).  This option
    clears the whole of that distance against the wider set, as it always
    has.

    Synthetic minimiser (x86-64, gcc layout).  `target` reads the global `ok`
    (0x1058) into a register, passes the global `g` (0x1050) to `helper`, writes
    a stack byte, folds the result into `ok` with a read-modify-write, stores
    that back, and only afterwards reads `g` again and calls `sink` (the second
    read is what keeps the call's INDIRECT version of `g` live):

      target: push rbx / sub rsp,0x10 / mov ebx,[ok] / mov rdi,[g]
              / call helper / mov byte [rsp],1 / and ebx,eax / mov [ok],ebx
              / movzx eax,byte [rsp] / mov [flag],al / mov rdi,[g] / call sink
              / add rsp,0x10 / pop rbx / ret
      helper: mov eax,5 / ret
      sink:   xor eax,eax / ret

    Control (not in the corpus): replacing `mov rdi,[g]` with `mov edi,7` makes
    plain foldcallret fold the call with no option at all, which is what pins
    the global operand as the blocker.

    The discount also needs the call's output to carry the callee's whole
    return value.  With no prototype for `helper`, its output is only the `eax`
    that `and ebx,eax` reads, while `helper` writes all of `rax`; the spill's
    32-bit declaration is then the only thing that narrows it, and a fold
    would drop that (an `unsigned long f()` returning 0xffffffff, folded into
    `f() == -1`, never compares equal).  Declaring `int4 helper(int8)` locks
    the output at 32 bits and the fold goes ahead.  The i386 `edx:eax` form of
    the same narrowing is kuna-foldcallretphi-join.xml.

    `target3` is gcc -O2's code for
      int r = fflush(stdout); gflag = (r == 0) && (a > 5);
    with `helper` for fflush, `g3` (0x1150) for stdout and `gflag` at 0x1158:

      target3: push rbx / mov ebx,edi / mov rdi,[g3] / call helper
               / test eax,eax / sete dl / xor eax,eax / cmp ebx,5 / pop rbx
               / setg al / and eax,edx / mov [gflag],eax / ret

    The call, both setcc and the and are one block, the and is a BOOL_AND,
    which C prints as `&&`, and the call's result reaches it as the right-hand
    operand.  Folding the call there gives `gflag = 5 < a0 && !helper(g3)`,
    which skips the call whenever a0 <= 5; the binary always makes it.  So
    the call keeps its spill.  (A result that reaches the left-hand operand is
    always evaluated and still folds.)

    Pass 1 (the default, no option command; `helper` undeclared): the output
    is narrower than the return register, so the spill stays -> #1, #2.
    Pass 2 (the default, `helper` declared `int4`): the call is folded into the
    read-modify-write and the spill is gone -> #3.
    Pass 3 (`target2`, the default): a global write between the call and its
    use still blocks the fold -> #4, #5.
    Pass 4 (`target3`, the default): the short-circuit keeps the spill -> #6, #7.
    Pass 5 (option foldcallretphi off, the opt-out): the call result spills to
    its own statement -> #1, #2.
    Pass 6 (`target3`, off): the spill -> #6.
-->
